WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.980
A new energy. A new audience. New stories.&nbsp;
Keep watching to find out the candid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.820
thoughts the Witcher showrunner and executive&nbsp;
producer had about the show’s big name exit.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.720
Fans of the expansive fantasy franchise&nbsp;
"The Witcher" are still reeling over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.440
news that Henry Cavill is departing the role&nbsp;
of protagonist Geralt of Rivia in the Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.700
series based upon it. After Cavill's final&nbsp;
outing in the part of Geralt in the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.420
upcoming third season, the character will&nbsp;
be played by Liam Hemsworth in Season 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:29.940
a casting choice that has fans divided (to say&nbsp;
the least). An online Change.org petition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.340
restore Cavill to the role has almost 275,000&nbsp;
signatures as of this video, and many viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.420
of the show are understandably skeptical of&nbsp;
recasting a show's main character mid-story.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.680
But no online petition is more powerful&nbsp;
than the Man of Steel, and Cavill is most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.640
likely exiting "The Witcher" due to his newly&nbsp;
reinvigorated commitment to playing Superman in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.360
the DC Universe – though some fans believe it's&nbsp;
because of his displeasure with the direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.380
of "The Witcher". Thus, fans won't be seeing&nbsp;
him again in the role of Geralt any time soon.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.542
"F--k"

00:00:58.542 --> 00:01:01.440
You can reasonably assume that&nbsp;
the "Witcher" showrunner Lauren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.040
Schmidt Hissrich also wishes Geralt&nbsp;
didn't have to be recast. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.520
in a recent interview with Decider, she&nbsp;
spoke optimistically and enthusiastically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.040
about the change and what it means&nbsp;
for the future of the series.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.120
Hissrich isn't worried about a disruption&nbsp;
of the show's creative momentum. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.400
she says she sees it as a positive. She said,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.320
"You know, I will say that change is also energy.&nbsp;
Change also brings [a] different energy. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.680
to us, that's just part of continuing&nbsp;
to roll forward into this franchise."

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.640
More open-minded fans of "The Witcher" might&nbsp;
understand what she means and will look forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.720
to seeing exactly what kind of new energy this&nbsp;
admittedly huge change will bring to the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.860
(and even most of the more close-minded&nbsp;
ones will probably still check it out ).

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.420
Recasting Geralt of Rivia wasn't the only subject&nbsp;
addressed during Lauren Schmidt Hissrich's Decider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.200
interview. With not just the upcoming third and&nbsp;
fourth seasons of "The Witcher" and "The Witcher:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.740
Blood Origin" on the way but also various&nbsp;
animated projects like "The Witcher:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.640
Nightmare of the Wolf," and even a family-friendly&nbsp;
animated series coming in the near future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.140
the "Witcher" franchise is growing incredibly&nbsp;
fast, and Hissrich also spoke on how she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.980
sees the series fitting into the fantasy&nbsp;
genre that so many fans love. She said,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.160
"One of the things that we believe&nbsp;
strongly ... is that fantasy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.920
actually for everyone. There have&nbsp;
been points in time, in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.700
that it's a very narrow audience. I think&nbsp;
all of [those] different access points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.780
if you will, all that does is make people fall in&nbsp;
love with the genre more, which is what we want."

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.300
She's also not worried about&nbsp;
the franchise hitting any kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.060
of critical mass regarding&nbsp;
audience endurance, saying:

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.140
"It's interesting because could there be&nbsp;
fatigue? I actually don't think so. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.740
the stories are so vast and also so varied,&nbsp;
and I think there's so many different ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.066
into the world that we could keep doing this&nbsp;
forever and we'd never run out of stories."

00:02:32.066 --> 00:02:35.460
"Have no fears. We've got stories for years."

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.580
Fans will get their chance to see the&nbsp;
next phase of Netflix's "The Witcher"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.700
saga when the four-episode "Blood&nbsp;
Origin" prequel miniseries hits the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.320
streaming platform on December 25th, 2022.&nbsp;
And Season 3 of "The Witcher" main series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.380
which will star Henry Cavill, is&nbsp;
set to come out sometime next year.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.800
"This is where we part ways, Bard. For good."

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.060
"Look I promised to change the public's&nbsp;
tune about you, at least allow me to try."

